<td id="S4.T1.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Qwen</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">24/30</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r">
<span id="S4.T1.5.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.5.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">[2, 3, 13, 21, 28, 29]</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Phi4</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">23/30</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">
<span id="S4.T1.5.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.5.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">[2, 3, 5, 13, 16, 21, 25]</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Baseline performance on AIME2025.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T2.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Questions</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Deepseek</td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">13/30</td>
<td id="S4.T2.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T2.5.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.5.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">[1, 4, 6, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17, 19, 21, 22, 25, 26, 27, 29]</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Baseline Performance ‣ 4 Results ‣ Certainty-Guided Reasoning in Large Language Models: A Dynamic Thinking Budget Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, questions 2, 3, 13, and 21 were incorrectly answered by all models, suggesting that they are particularly challenging and may require deeper or more complex reasoning paths.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Performance with Certainty-Guided Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Deepseek, applying CGR with a threshold of 0.97 led to a one-question improvement on AIME24. On AIME25, in the number of correct questions was approximately the same, since for question <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> the model never stopped thinking,</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If we take a closer look at the incorrect question difference, we can see that, for example, question 13, is no longer marked as incorrect, meaning that the model did not have enough certainty in its answer. This can be seen as a positive, as we discuss later in the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Grade</span> section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance with certainty-guided early exit on AIME2024.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T3.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Questions</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Deepseek</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">16/30</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T3.5.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T3.5.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">[2, 3, 4, 8, 17, 21, 28]</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance with certainty-guided early exit on AIME2025.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T4.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Questions</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Deepseek</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">12/30</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T4.5.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:213.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T4.5.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">[1, 3, 12, 17, 19, 21, 22, 25]</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Token-Level Dynamics in Deepseek</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further analyzed Deepseek’s token-by-token performance on AIME2024 and AIME2025, tracking how many questions were correctly or incorrectly answered at each 1k token budget.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2509.07820v1/asdasdaza.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/160;" width="215" height="160" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F1.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AIME2024</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2509.07820v1/sdfw.png" id="S4.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/160;" width="215" height="160" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F1.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AIME2025</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Deepseek accuracy as a function of thinking budgets</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that most gains occurred within the first 10k tokens, with diminishing returns afterward. The jump at 25k tokens on the AIME2025 suggests that some answers benefit from long-term refinement, but these are the minority. Overall, this supports our design choice to probe every 1k tokens and exit early when certainty is high, optimizing both accuracy and compute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Grade comparison</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further evaluate the usefulness of certainty estimates, we introduce a metric called <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Grade</span>, inspired by real-world exams that penalize incorrect answers. The Grade reflects the performance of a hypothetical user who only answers questions they are confident about, skipping uncertain ones. Specifically, we define:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Score}(\textsc{Query})=\begin{cases}\hfill+1&amp;\text{if correct answer}\\
\hfill 0&amp;\text{if abstention}\\
\hfill-c&amp;\text{if wrong answer}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Query</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>if correct answer</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>if abstention</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>if wrong answer</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Score}(\textsc{Query})=\begin{cases}\hfill+1&amp;\text{if correct answer}\\
\hfill 0&amp;\text{if abstention}\\
\hfill-c&amp;\text{if wrong answer}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Score}(\textsc{Dataset})=\textsc{Total Correct}-c\times\textsc{Total Wrong}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Dataset</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Total Correct</mtext><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Total Wrong</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Score}(\textsc{Dataset})=\textsc{Total Correct}-c\times\textsc{Total Wrong}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Grade}=\text{TotalCorrect}-c\cdot\text{TotalIncorrect}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Grade</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TotalCorrect</mtext><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>TotalIncorrect</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Grade}=\text{TotalCorrect}-c\cdot\text{TotalIncorrect}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a penalty coefficient, tested with values <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, representing different exam policies: no penalty, mild penalty, and strong penalty respectively. A higher grade indicates that the model not only answers correctly more often but also avoids making confident errors. The baseline is the grade the model would get if they answered all questions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2509.07820v1/wsda.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/124;" width="153" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2509.07820v1/asdasz.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/124;" width="153" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><img src="2509.07820v1/cgf.png" id="S4.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/124;" width="153" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.fig3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Grade with varying <span id="S4.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">c</span> on the AIME2025</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The results reinforce the value of early stopping driven by certainty, especially under penalized settings. While traditional accuracy metrics treat all answers equally, the Grade metric rewards precision and discourages confident guessing—better aligning with real testing scenarios where mistakes carry weight.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Multi-Seed Evaluation on AIME25</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the stability of Certainty-Guided Reasoning (CGR), we extended our experiments on the AIME2025 dataset to run across 64 different random seeds. This significantly increases the robustness of our evaluation, offering a more comprehensive estimate of performance variability compared to the single-seed runs presented earlier. By averaging over a large number of seeds, we reduce the risk of drawing conclusions from outlier behaviors and ensure that both CGR and the resulting Grade metrics reflect consistent trends rather than seed-specific artifacts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across seeds, we observed that CGR maintains consistent accuracy and Grade improvements. While the absolute number of correct answers fluctuates due to random initialization, the variance is significantly reduced, demonstrating that CGR is not overly sensitive to seed choice. This high-seed evaluation also helps us better understand the generalization behavior of LLM-based reasoning techniques, providing a stronger foundation for comparing methods and validating improvements. Moreover, it allows us to more confidently attribute performance gains to the method itself, rather than to stochastic effects in the model’s inference dynamics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/zzaz.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/189;" width="381" height="189" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cumulative Avg. Predictions Threshold 96</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/AS.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/189;" width="381" height="189" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cumulative Avg. Predictions Threshold 99</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/cc.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/189;" width="381" height="189" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Avg. Grade over Thinking Tokens Penalty 0.5 Threshold 99</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across seeds, we observed that CGR maintains consistent accuracy and Grade improvements. While the absolute number of correct answers fluctuates due to random initialization, the variance is significantly reduced, demonstrating that CGR is not overly sensitive to seed choice.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean Accuracy (std)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grade Score</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline (Deepseek, 64 seeds)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">14.3/30</td>
<td id="S4.T5.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">–</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">CGR (Deepseek, 64 seeds)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">14.1/30</td>
<td id="S4.T5.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">–</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Grade (c = 0.25)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">–</td>
<td id="S4.T5.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Baseline = 10, CGR = 10</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Grade (c = 0.5)</th>
<td id="S4.T5.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">–</td>
<td id="S4.T5.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Baseline = 6.2, CGR = 6.3</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.T5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of Baseline and CGR methods over 64 seeds on the AIME2025 dataset. Accuracy is reported as mean correct answers out of 30 with standard deviation. Grade scores are shown for two different confidence thresholds <math id="S4.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/ZZ.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/189;" width="381" height="189" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Avg. Grade over Thinking Tokens</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results confirm that CGR not only improves accuracy but also reduces the likelihood of confidently answers, especially under penalized grading scenarios. Importantly, the updated Grade values show that CGR yields more robust exam-like performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Token Savings Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An additional benefit of Certainty-Guided Reasoning (CGR) is its ability to reduce unnecessary computation. By allowing the model to terminate early once sufficient certainty is reached, CGR can substantially cut down the number of tokens generated per query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across 64 seeds on AIME2025, CGR achieved significant savings:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of token savings across certainty thresholds on AIME2025 (64 seeds).</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T6.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T6.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Tokens Saved</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. per Seed</span></th>
<th id="S4.T6.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. per Question</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T6.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">0.96</th>
<td id="S4.T6.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">3,380,578</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">52,821</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">1,760</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">0.97</th>
<td id="S4.T6.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">3,081,690</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">48,151</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1,605</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row">0.98</th>
<td id="S4.T6.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">2,739,761</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">42,808</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">1,426</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b">0.99</th>
<td id="S4.T6.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_b">2,042,389</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_b">31,912</td>
<td id="S4.T6.5.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_b">1,063</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results clearly demonstrate a trade-off between certainty threshold and token savings. At lower thresholds (0.96–0.97), the model exits reasoning earlier, yielding the largest savings: over 3.3M tokens saved in total, or around 53k per seed. However, these thresholds risk premature stopping, potentially lowering reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This trend is expected: as the certainty threshold increases, the model requires stronger evidence before halting, which naturally means it continues reasoning for longer. Since higher confidence often comes only after exploring more reasoning steps, fewer tokens are saved at stricter thresholds. In other words, the more certainty we demand, the more the model “keeps trying,” reducing the opportunity for early exits but yielding more reliable answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As the threshold increases, the model becomes more conservative, reasoning longer before halting. This reduces token savings, with the 0.99 threshold saving over 2.0M tokens total (31.9k per seed), but provides stronger guarantees of correctness by only stopping when confidence is very high.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, this means system designers can tune the certainty threshold based on application needs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficiency-focused settings (e.g., large-scale batch inference) benefit from a lower threshold like 0.96, maximizing savings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy- or safety-critical settings (e.g., exams, law, finance) are better served with a higher threshold like 0.99, sacrificing some efficiency for reliability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/wer.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/255;" width="381" height="255" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Tokens Saved per Seed per Question Threshold 99</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">These results demonstrate that CGR not only maintains accuracy (14.1 vs. 14.3 baseline average) but also reduces computational cost, making it a more efficient approach for large-scale deployment. Importantly, this efficiency gain compounds when reasoning is applied across many queries, highlighting the practicality of certainty-guided early exits in real-world systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.7 </span>Question Ranking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The per-question distribution of token savings also provides insight into problem difficulty.
Questions where the model saves many tokens are those where it reaches high certainty quickly,
suggesting they are relatively <em id="S4.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">easy</em>. Conversely, questions with little or no savings are
likely <em id="S4.SS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">harder</em>, requiring extended reasoning before the model becomes confident.
Thus, token savings can serve as a proxy for ordering questions by difficulty for LLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07820v1/zxc.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/227;" width="381" height="227" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Token savings per question on AIME2025.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond difficulty estimation, this type of analysis enables several impactful applications. It supports adaptive allocation of compute by directing more resources to questions that historically require longer reasoning. It also informs curriculum design for training, helping identify problem types that consistently demand deeper reasoning and using them for targeted fine-tuning. Additionally, it enables hybrid inference strategies, such as combining fast exits on easier questions with budget-constrained reasoning on more complex ones. Ultimately, the token-savings distribution offers more than just efficiency metrics — it provides a valuable window into the reasoning landscape of large language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we proposed Certainty-Guided Reasoning (CGR), a dynamic framework that enables large reasoning language models to allocate their thinking budget more effectively by incorporating internal measures of confidence. Rather than adhering to a token limit or terminating reasoning prematurely, our approach allows the model to continue thinking when uncertain and stop early when confident—striking a balance between computational efficiency and solution reliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our method combines three key components: (1) periodic probing of intermediate outputs to evaluate certainty, (2) a fixed certainty threshold calibrated through empirical analysis (set at 0.97), and (3) budget forcing to override premature halting when confidence is low. This integrated design provides the model with opportunities for self-correction and refinement while conserving resources when appropriate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation on the AIME2024 and AIME2025 datasets shows that CGR can reduce token usage while maintaining accuracy. Token-level analysis revealed that most reasoning improvements occur within the first 10,000 tokens, justifying our choice of periodic probing and early exit thresholds. The introduction of the Grade metric—penalizing incorrect high-confidence answers—further illustrates the value of CGR in high-stakes settings where abstention is preferable to confident error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, our multi-seed experiments across 64 runs confirm that CGR is stable and not overly sensitive to stochastic initialization. While absolute accuracy fluctuates slightly, variance is significantly reduced, and Grade improvements remain consistent. These results provide stronger evidence that CGR’s benefits derive from the method itself rather than seed-specific artifacts. Moreover, the token savings analysis shows that CGR can save millions of tokens across seeds, offering substantial efficiency gains with tunable trade-offs between certainty thresholds and computational cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These findings underscore the utility of certainty as a first-class signal in language model inference. By enabling models to “know when they know,” we reduce the incidence of overconfident hallucinations and improve trustworthiness. This has implications not just for academic benchmarks, but also for real-world applications in domains like finance, law, and education, where logical correctness is critical and computational resources may be limited.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Several promising directions remain open for exploration. First, although we used a fixed certainty threshold across all problems, dynamic or problem-specific thresholding may yield better results, especially if calibrated online or using external signals like input complexity. Second, while our current probing uses the same model for both reasoning and certainty estimation, future implementations could explore hybrid systems where smaller, faster models assess certainty—reducing overhead in API-based or real-time settings. We also consider a future mechanism for flushing and reinitializing reasoning traces when stagnation is detected, leveraging sampling randomness to explore alternate solution paths. Third, the certainty metric could be refined beyond token-level probabilities—for example, incorporating entropy, variance across sampled trajectories, or alignment with external verifiers. Finally, while our experiments focused on math problems, the CGR paradigm could extend to other reasoning-intensive domains such as coding, commonsense reasoning, and open-domain question answering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Ultimately, our findings advocate for a shift in how LLMs reason: not just reasoning more, but reasoning smartly. By making internal confidence actionable, certainty-guided reasoning enables more adaptive, reliable, and efficient use of language models, paving the way for more trustworthy and resource-aware AI systems.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
